  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity and resilience of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">(a) The National Assembly adopted an amendment to the Labour Code, adding an article on the definition of forced labour which widens the scope, coverage and quality of services provided by labour inspections in tackling forced labour (October 2022).

(b) A Government draft law repealing the need of labour inspectors to prior notify employers of inspection visits (with 3 working days in advance), was submitted to the Parliament. The Inspection Bodies Coordination Bureau (IBCB) is promoting an extension in the scope of application of the draft law to all inspection bodies and not just to labour inspectorate prior to its formal approval. The ILO conducted a legal and procedural gap assessment of the Labour Administration and Labour Inspection Systems of Armenia, and provided technical recommendations to the report &quot;Assessment of the Labour Inspection System in the Republic of Armenia&quot;. 

Technical support was provided to the formulation and implementation of a Strategic Compliance Plan by the Labour Inspectorate in Armenia.

Technical recommendations and advice provided to the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs of Armenia on how to address labour inspection and OSH in the Labour and Social Protection Umbrella Strategy of Armenia.

Advice provided to HLIB on formulation and implementation of the roadmap aimed at further aligning national legislation and practices with ILO C81.
In partnership with ITC-ILO and the Association of Judges of Armenia, development of a course on domestic labour law and ILS and their application on judicial decisions.

The ILO developed an analysis and recommendations on alternative dispute resolution and guidelines for labour grievance handling.</narrative>
   </description>
